        <div id="patterns" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Type of Laser Level Do I Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Match the beam pattern to the work: a cross-line for one wall at a time, a 360 or three-plane laser for whole rooms, a dot or plumb laser for moving points between floor and ceiling, and a rotary laser for outdoor grading.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What does a cross-line laser do?</h3>

          <p>
            A <strong>cross-line laser</strong> projects a level line and a plumb line that meet in a cross on the wall in front of it. The lines typically fan out across 110 to 130 degrees, enough to cover one wall or parts of two. It is the pattern for hanging pictures, shelves, curtain rods and televisions, for setting chair rail and wainscoting, and for lining up a backsplash.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When is a 360 or three-plane laser worth it?</h3>

          <p>
            A <strong>360-degree laser</strong> carries at least the horizontal line all the way around the room. A <strong>three-plane</strong> laser, also sold as 3x360 or 3D, adds two vertical 360-degree planes set at right angles to each other. From one position in the middle of a room you get a level line on every wall and two plumb lines crossing the floor and ceiling, which also gives you square. Cabinet installers, framers and tile setters work this way.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What are dot and plumb lasers for?</h3>

          <p>
            <strong>Dot or plumb lasers</strong> project points rather than lines, usually straight up, straight down and sometimes level in several directions. They carry a point from floor to ceiling or from one wall to another, which is exactly what centering a light fixture over a table or lining up recessed lights requires. Many cross-line lasers add plumb dots, as the Klein and Stabila picks do.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When do you need a rotary laser?</h3>

          <p>
            When the work is outside. A <strong>rotary laser</strong> spins one beam at high speed to form a plane that a detector can pick up hundreds of feet away, which makes it the tool for grading, foundations, fences and decks. Indoors it can set a drop ceiling across a large basement, though in small rooms a line laser is quicker.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why does fan angle matter?</h3>

          <p>
            Fan angle tells you how much wall a single line covers. Set a cross-line with a 120-degree fan 8 feet from a wall and it draws a line roughly 27 feet long, enough to cross most rooms; narrower fans mean moving the tool more often. Three-plane lasers sidestep the issue because each plane is a full circle, but they are bigger and use more power to do it.
          </p>

