        <div id="green-red" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Is a Green Laser Level Better Than Red?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For bright rooms and long distances, yes. For short indoor jobs where you control the light, a red laser is cheaper, runs longer on batteries and does the work just as well.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why does a green beam look brighter?</h3>

          <p>
            Human eyes are most sensitive to light in the green part of the spectrum, so a green laser with the same power output appears several times brighter than a red one; manufacturers often put the gain at up to about four times the visibility. On the job, that means a green line stays readable across a room with sun coming through a window, while a red line can wash out beyond 15 or 20 feet.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When is red the smarter buy?</h3>

          <p>
            Red keeps some real advantages. It costs less, draws less power and runs longer on a set of batteries, and red diodes tend to cope better with temperature swings. For hanging art in the evening, working in a basement or any job within a dozen feet of the wall, a red cross-line is fully adequate and often the better purchase.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Can either color work outside?</h3>

          <p>
            Not at any real distance in direct sun; daylight overpowers the beam whatever its color. Outdoor work calls for a laser that supports a detector, which senses the pulsed beam electronically even when your eyes cannot see it. Many line lasers offer a pulse mode for this, so confirm it before buying if you plan to work outdoors.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Does your eyesight change the choice?</h3>

          <p>
            It can. Some people simply find green easier to see, and people with certain types of color vision deficiency see red poorly. If red laser pointers have given you trouble, that alone is a good reason to pay for green. Whatever the color, never look into the beam or aim it at anyone's eyes. Most of these tools are Class 2 or Class 3R lasers, and the warning labels are there for a reason.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SHORT VERSION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Green for bright rooms and large spaces; red for small jobs and tight budgets.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">When most of your work stays on one wall and you can control the light, red saves money. When you work in daylight, across whole rooms or in new construction with open windows, green earns its premium.</p>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How does beam color affect battery life?</h3>

          <p>
            Green diodes pull more current, so a green cross-line on AA cells may last only a fraction as long as a red one on the same batteries. That is one reason many green lasers have switched to rechargeable lithium packs or tool-platform batteries. If you go green, plan on a rechargeable pack or a steady supply of fresh cells.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="accuracy-range" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Accurate Does a Laser Level Need to Be?
          </h2>

          <p>
            For decorating and shelving, a rating of about an eighth of an inch at 30 feet is plenty. Long cabinet runs, large tile floors and drop ceilings justify a tool rated closer to a sixteenth of an inch at 30 feet.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What does "1/8 inch at 30 feet" mean?</h3>

          <p>
            Accuracy is normally given as a deviation over a distance, such as plus or minus an eighth of an inch at 30 feet. In other words, a line projected 30 feet away may sit up to an eighth of an inch above or below true level. Across the few feet of a shelf on one wall, that error is far smaller than the width of the line itself. Across a 20-foot run of kitchen cabinets, it starts to count.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where do most laser levels fall?</h3>

          <p>
            Most quality line lasers land between roughly a sixteenth and an eighth of an inch at 30 feet. That gap matters on long cabinet runs, tile across big floors and drop ceilings, and hardly at all for decorating. Treat any tool that lists no accuracy figure at all with suspicion.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What is the difference between visible and detector range?</h3>

          <p>
            Range is quoted two ways. <strong>Visible range</strong> is how far your eyes can follow the line, usually stated for dim indoor conditions. <strong>Detector range</strong> is how far a receiver can pick the beam up, often several times farther. When a spec sheet says 330 feet, it generally means with a detector; in a bright room the visible range may be a fraction of that.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Does line width affect accuracy?</h3>

          <p>
            In practice, yes. Lines widen with distance, and it is easy to mark the top edge on one wall and the middle on the next. Choose one edge or the center and stick with it. Outdoors and on long indoor runs, a detector removes the guesswork because it reads the center of the beam.
          </p>

